---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Trust & safety"
  description="Understand candidate drafts, certification, publisher installers, held and revoked releases, and RepoReady's download and review boundaries."
  canonicalPath="/trust-safety/"
>
  <header class="page-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Know what a status means</p>
    <h1>Trust & safety</h1>
    <p>
      Review status should help you make an informed decision. It is never a
      guarantee that software is free of bugs or malicious behavior.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        <strong>Current availability:</strong> zero certified releases. Catalog
        entries are research drafts, not tested or approved downloads.
      </p>
    </div>
    <h2>From a candidate to a reviewed release</h2>
    <p>
      A candidate description explains an upstream project and records known
      requirements and unanswered questions. Editorial stages such as
      researching or awaiting license review describe that work; they do not
      authorize installation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before a release can be published, its exact version and artifact must
      have reviewed source and license information, screenshots and credits,
      compatibility and resource disclosures, network and privacy notes, API
      costs, limitations, and certification evidence.
    </p>
    <h2>Release statuses</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>
        <strong>Candidate draft</strong>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Research is in progress. No RepoReady testing or approved download is
        implied.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <strong>Ready</strong>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        The exact release has certification evidence for the supported
        configuration and core tasks. Check the testing date and limitations.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <strong>Publisher installer</strong>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        The distribution uses an upstream publisher's installer. It still needs
        the required review and testing; publisher origin alone is not
        certification.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <strong>Held</strong>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        A release is temporarily unavailable while a concern is investigated.
        Installation and download actions are suppressed.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <strong>Revoked</strong>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Approval has been withdrawn. Download and installation actions are
        suppressed. Review any published guidance before continuing to use a
        previously downloaded copy.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <strong>Unavailable</strong>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        No usable approved release is currently offered for the selected app or
        configuration.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <h2>Standalone apps and the managed runtime</h2>
    <p>
      An approved standalone distribution can be downloaded directly from the
      website and used without installing RepoReady. This remains possible even
      if the desktop client is unavailable. Apps that require RepoReady's
      managed runtime must disclose that dependency on their detail page.
    </p>
    <h2>What signatures and hashes establish</h2>
    <p>
      A verified signature identifies the approved catalog metadata. A matching
      artifact hash checks that the downloaded bytes match the reviewed release.
      Neither proves that a program is harmless, and a hash displayed on a page
      alone does not establish approval.
    </p>
    <p>
      Downloads will point to exact approved artifacts rather than mutable
      “latest” links. A static page or previously downloaded file may remain in
      a cache after a release is held or revoked; status changes cannot remove a
      file already on your computer.
    </p>
    <h2>Report a concern</h2>
    <p>
      Use the <a href="/support/">support and reporting guidance</a> for broken
      links, inaccurate disclosures, licensing concerns, or suspected unsafe
      behavior. Do not include passwords, API keys, private files, or exploit
      details in a public issue.
    </p>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
